icy."}, {"sourceSlug": "berne", "sourceName": "Bern", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "swiss-peoples-party", "targetName": "Swiss People's Party", "context": "Bern serves as the political center where the Swiss People's Party was founded and continues to operate."}, {"sourceSlug": "populism-in-europe", "sourceName": "Populism in Europe", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "swiss-peoples-party", "targetName": "Swiss People's Party", "context": "The rise of populism across Europe has created an environment in which the Swiss People's Party could flourish and influence broader political trends."}], "places": [{"name": "Bern, Switzerland", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "Zurich, Switzerland", "role": "Major City"}, {"name": "Geneva, Switzerland", "role": "Cultural Hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Swiss People's Party", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "swiss-peoples-party"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q385258", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss_People%27s_Party"], "tags": ["Switzerland", "political party", "Europe", "Political Parties & Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Swiss People's Party contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Swiss People's Party has reshaped national discourse on immigration and identity, securing nearly 30% of the federal vote in 2015 and influencing similar movements across Europe.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-15T01:06:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29380435826", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Political party in switzerland (svp/udc). Swiss People's Party is a political party in Bern, established in 1971-09-22. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Swiss People's Party (SVP/UDC), founded on September 22, 1971, emerged as a pivotal right-wing political force in Switzerland, with its roots tracing back to earlier conservative movements. Originating in Bern, the SVP has carved out a significant niche in the political landscape of Western Europe, advocating for Swiss nationalism, direct democracy, and a skeptical stance towards immigration. Key figures have included prominent politicians such as Christoph Blocher, who has been instrumental in shaping its policies and public image.

The party's ascent can be marked by its participation in various federal elections, where it has consistently secured a substantial share of the vote, peaking at 29.4% in the 2015 federal elections. This success has allowed the SVP to influence key legislative decisions and dominate discussions on immigration policy and national identity, particularly through referendums that reflect the party's core values. The SVP's campaigns often harness populist rhetoric, emphasizing themes of sovereignty and cultural preservation, which resonate with a significant portion of the Swiss electorate.

